1️⃣ Fixed Window:
===================
windowStart = floor(t / W) * W

if (windowStart != prevWindow):
    count = 0

allow if count < limit

2️⃣ Sliding Window Log
====================
S = { ti | ti ∈ timestamps and ti > t - W }

allow if |S| < limit

on allow:
    timestamps.add(t)
3️⃣ Token Bucket (AWS style)
====================
Δt = t - lastRefillTime

tokens = min(C, tokens + r * Δt)

allow if tokens ≥ 1

if allowed:
    tokens = tokens - 1

lastRefillTime = t
4️⃣ Leaky Bucket (for later file)
====================
queue = max(0, queue - drainRate * Δt)

allow if queue < capacity

if allowed:
    queue = queue + 1
5️⃣ Sliding Window Counter (approximation)
====================
let currentWindowStart = floor(t / W) * W
let previousWindowStart = currentWindowStart - W

weight = (t - currentWindowStart) / W

effectiveCount =
    count_current +
    count_previous * (1 - weight)

allow if effectiveCount < limit

🎯 Intuition comparison (1-liners)
====================
Strategy	       Intuition
Fixed Window	   “How many in this second?”
Sliding Log	       “How many in last 1 second from now?”
Token Bucket	   “Do I have tokens to spend?”
Leaky Bucket	   “Is my queue full?”
Sliding Counter	   “Weighted blend of last + current window”
🚀 Interview ready one-liner

Fixed window is simple but bursty, sliding log is accurate but heavy, token bucket allows controlled bursts with O(1) memory,
and sliding window counter gives a good approximation with low cost.

